window.docContents[22].resolve({"/Maps/#section-morphisms":{"contents":"Graph homomorphisms, embeddings, and isomorphisms are the structure-preserving maps\nbetween graphs. Unlike comap, which takes a plain function and builds a new graph,\na morphism is data relating two given graphs: a function between their vertex types\ntogether with a proof that it respects adjacency. We will see below how the two ideas\nare related.\n\nBefore looking at morphisms in detail we note that they are all defined as abbrevs\nfor the corresponding Rel* types in Mathlib. This means that occasionally when\nworking with these maps we will use results that refer to RelHom, RelEmbedding or\nRelIso.\n\nLeft: a homomorphism from C_4 onto K_2, identifying opposite vertices. Centre: an embedding of a path, which sends its non-adjacent ends a and c to the non-adjacent w and y. Right: an isomorphism between two drawings of C_4\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps","header":"5.3. Morphisms","id":"/Maps/#section-morphisms"}});